了\" — placeholders: first %s=shooter name, second=his/her. Target: \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" — hmm, this reads as \"[shooter]的[his/her]射击打偏了\" = \"[shooter]'s [his/her] shot missed\" — that's wrong ordering semantics? Source: \"X misses his shot.\" = shooter misses his/her own shot. Target: \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" — first %s gets shooter name, second %s gets his/her → \"Aldric的他的射击打偏了\"? That's odd. Actually the Chinese would render as \"[Name]的[他的]射击打偏了\" which is redundant/wrong: should be \"[Name]打偏了他的射击\" or similar. Hmm. But his_her in Chinese localization—maybe second %s maps to something. Actually the placeholders must be filled in order: first %s = srcname, second %s = his_her. In Chinese \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" the first %s is possessor, second %s is... also possessor. \"张三的他的射击\" is ungrammatical. Hmm, but maybe the translation intends \"打偏\" with the second %s being the target? No—source second %s is his/her (shooter's own). So target structure: \"X的他的射击打偏了\" — grammatically awkward but conveys meaning. Is this a defect? Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe reconsider: perhaps the Chinese localization replaces his_her with something else? No, the runtime passes string.his_her(self). The target string has two %s. First = shooter name, second = his/her. \"张三的他的射击打偏了\" — redundant possessive. A better translation: \"%s 打偏了 %s 射击\" doesn't work either. Actually a natural Chinese rendering would be \"%s 的射击打偏了\" using only one placeholder, but they must keep two placeholders? Not necessarily—tformat/logSeen uses both args; if the string has fewer placeholders it's fine in Lua? format(\"%s的射击打偏了\", srcname, his_her) — extra args are ignored in Lua string.format. Actually Lua string.format ignores extra arguments. So dropping the second placeholder is possible. But keeping it as \"的%s射击\" is awkward.\n\nHmm, is this a \"material defect\"? The meaning is preserved roughly: shooter's shot missed. But \"张三的他的射击\" is wrong Chinese. Actually wait—maybe it's fine if his/her localizes... no, his_her returns \"his\