ey intent—the talent info does. Not material.\n\nFor the placeholder redundancy: \"张三的他的射击打偏了\". Hmm, actually in Chinese, \"他的\" as second placeholder... The rendered string would be like \"兽人的他的射击打偏了\". That's clearly defective Chinese. But is it established only with context? We know second %s = his/her from context. Surface-visible: you can compare source \"%s misses %s shot\" — second %s modifies \"shot\" as possessive pronoun (\"his shot\"). In target \"%s的%s射击\", second %s also modifies \"射击\" possessively. So a reviewer without context can see the structure: \"A misses B shot\" → \"A的B射击打偏了\". If B is a pronoun referring to A, \"A的B射击\" is redundant. But without context, could B be the target? \"X misses Y shot\" doesn't parse that way in English grammatically (\"misses his shot\"). So it's arguably surface-visible that second placeholder is a possessive pronoun. Hmm, but surface reading of \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" alone: maybe they intended first=shooter, second=target? \"X的Y射击打偏了\" doesn't make sense with Y=target either.\n\nHonestly I think the cleanest interpretation: the translation is fine functionally—\"X的他的射击打偏了\" is awkward. But many Chinese translations of ToME handle pronouns by... hmm.\n\nLet me weigh: The task says provide exactly one label per item with evidence. The safest defensible reading: For RFR-0140, the placeholder order in the target is reversed relative to English semantics? Source: \"%s misses %s shot\" — arg1 = shooter (subject), arg2 = pronoun (possessive). Target: \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" — first slot = possessive of shooter, second slot = ? The second placeholder in the target occupies the position modifying 射击, which matches arg2 = pronoun possessive. So order preserved. The rendering \"X的他的射击打偏了\" — redundant but meaning preserved. I'll call it... Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe I'm overthinking. Perhaps in this localization, his_her for Chinese might be handled elsewhere. But context explicitly says \"second=his/her\". Given the rendered output \"张三的他的射击