n duplicating X — visible from English grammar. Hmm, it's borderline. Honestly, I'm torn.\n\nLet me reconsider once more whether the target is actually defective or fine. Alternative reading: maybe the Chinese is intentionally \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" where the second %s is meant to be filled by the localized pronoun and the phrase reads like \"他的射击\" attached... \"张三的他的射击打偏了\" — no way to read this as good Chinese.\n\nBut wait — maybe there's another reading: \"打偏了\" — maybe the intended full reading is \"%s 的 %s 射击打偏了\" = \"[Name]'s [startling] shot went astray\"? Could second %s be an adjective? No, runtime passes his_her.\n\nOK here's the thing: this is a \"reference\" shard likely used to calibrate. The mixture: mechanics items where English is stale and Chinese follows code (CLEAN via context — but wait, per the label definitions, if surface shows a mismatch but context shows it's correct, the label is CLEAN since \"no material translation defect supported by the supplied evidence\"). For 0140, hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider 0140 once more. Perhaps the intended reading is different: \"Startling Shot\" — the shooter deliberately fires wide. Log: \"Aldric misses his shot.\" Chinese: \"Aldric的他的射击打偏了\". Hmm what if the second %s slot was intended to be the localized pronoun but placed as \"的%s射击\" reading \"他的射击\" as a unit — \"张三的 他的射击\" — no, the 的 already attaches.\n\nAlternatively maybe the translator intended: \"%s的%s射击打偏了\" where first %s = shooter name... and they thought second %s = \"his/her\" giving \"他的射击\" — like \"Aldric的\" + \"他的射击\"? That's \"Aldric's his shot\" — wrong.\n\nI'll make a decision: label CONTEXT_DEPENDENT_DEFECT for RFR-0140, with atom stating the redundant double-possessive when placeholders are filled (second %s = his/her per context), rendering \"X的他的射击打偏了\". Cite EVIDENCE-RFR-0140 sha256: 1a2229d0b3f207c132fb9c0e95bcdac839ad11087cc214dda2ba873fa5f2ae75.\n\nHmm, but wait — is this defect \"material\"? A redundant pronoun making the log 